<p>{% person "george-peoples" %}George Peoples{% endperson %}, then a Bismarck resident, purchased lots 14 and 15 of Block 6 in Mandan from the {% place "northern-pacific-railroad" %}Northern Pacific Railroad{% endplace %} for {% inflation 375, 1881 %} in July 1881. He built a hotel in 1882–1883 for {% inflation 23500, 1882 %}, using brick manufactured just east of Mandan. Located at the northeastern corner of Main Street and Collins Avenue, it became the city's second three-story building, after the {% place "inter-ocean-hotel" %}InterOcean Hotel{% endplace %} four blocks west, and its first three-story brick building. The hotel opened in April 1882.</p>

<p>The walls were five bricks thick on the first floor, four on the second, and three on the third. Floor timbers measuring three inches by 12 inches came from a dismantled Minnesota flour mill. {% place "bingenheimer-and-taylors-drug-store" %}Bingenheimer and Taylor's Drug and Fancy Goods{% endplace %} operated in the ground-floor storefront along Main Street, while {% place "s-smith-dry-goods" %}S. Smith Dry Goods{% endplace %} occupied the Stark Avenue, now Collins Avenue, side. {% place "gussners-meat-market" %}Gussner's Meat Market{% endplace %} stood east of the hotel and supplied its dining room. A second floor was later added to the market building, but an air shaft between the structures allowed the hotel windows to remain usable.</p>

<p>Peoples later sold the hotel to {% person "pat-mcginley" %}Pat McGinley{% endperson %}, who renamed it the McGinley. Before air conditioning, families visited the hotel's dining room for home-cooked summer meals without having to heat their own houses.</p>

