<p>{% person "jane-lauscher" %}Jane Lauscher{% endperson %}, widow of a successful Red River Valley bonanza farmer, purchased the hotel and operated it from approximately 1908 through 1916. She undertook a major remodeling of its rooms and marketed the hotel to railroad workers and area ranchers.</p>

<p>{% person "nels-hanson-romer" %}Nels{% endperson %} and {% person "lillian-romer" %}Lillian Romer{% endperson %} purchased the business in 1918. Nels served as a city commissioner in 1922–1923 and later became Mandan's police chief. Lillian continued operating the hotel briefly after he was shot and killed in the line of duty in December 1926.</p>

<p>In 1929, {% person "otto-gussner" %}Otto Gussner{% endperson %} opened a radiator shop behind the hotel, in space where the city had stored fire trucks until a new {% place "mandan-city-hall-before-1928" %}city hall{% endplace %} was built. The shop moved to the rear of the lot along the alley when the hotel was remodeled in 1947.</p>

<p>The building operated exclusively as a hotel until 1968. Other tenants over the years included attorney {% person "john-sullivan" %}John Sullivan{% endperson %}, Taylor Drug, {% place "white-star-cafe" %}White Star Cafe{% endplace %}, {% place "john-sands-bar" %}John Sand's Bar{% endplace %}, {% place "st-elmos-restaurant" %}St. Elmo's Restaurant{% endplace %} in the basement, {% place "mandan-news" %}<em>The Mandan News</em>{% endplace %}, and {% place "crescent-printing" %}Crescent Printing{% endplace %}. A Christian coffeehouse occupied the main floor for a decade before the final tenant, {% place "ratz-lounge" %}Ratz Lounge{% endplace %}. The building was demolished in April and May 2007.</p>

